Subject: What does "partcomp derivatives not optimized" mean?
Message-ID: <hVoq8_sB6Go.ugrABjprBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/hVoq8_sB6Go/m/ugrABjprBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Phil!

I'm a new mirt user and I'm fitting a multidimensional IRT model with 2 factors. My aim is to do invariance testing and I wanted to check the model fit of my configural model using M2( ) function but it shows the following message: "partcomp derivatives not optimized". Would you explain me what does it mean? Should I be worried about?

I'll appreciate your help.

Best regards.

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Fri Apr 29 06:14:49 2016
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Fri, 29 Apr 2016 06:14:49 +0000
Subject: Re: What does "partcomp derivatives not optimized" mean?
Message-ID: <hVoq8_sB6Go.6nX90Y5sBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <hVoq8_sB6Go.ugrABjprBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <hVoq8_sB6Go.ugrABjprBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/hVoq8_sB6Go/m/6nX90Y5sBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It just means that they are computed much less efficiently than other models where I've worked out the derivatives analytically (uses high precision numerical calculations instead). There's nothing wrong with them, just slower.

Phil

